{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"For a UK-resident individual holding the ETF outside an ISA or pension:\n\n- **Undistributed income:** The fund’s **excess reportable income (ERI)** is taxable even though no cash is received. It is normally treated as received **six months after the reporting period ends**, for investors holding shares at that period-end. It generally attracts dividend tax treatment; qualifying bond funds instead produce interest income. Relevant allowances may apply.\n- **Disposal:** If UK reporting-fund status applied throughout ownership, disposal gains generally fall under **Capital Gains Tax**, rather than being offshore income gains taxed as income. ERI previously brought into account for income tax increases the allowable acquisition cost, preventing double taxation. Normal CGT exemptions and loss rules apply.\n\n**Worth knowing:** An accumulating ETF can create an annual income-tax liability without paying you cash."}}
